{
  "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
  "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Charles L. McCloud",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. McCLOUD: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The First Circuit held below that district courts are barred from considering intervening law and facts when deciding whether to impose a reduced sentence under the First Step Act. That holding conflicts with the law's text, and it flouts background principles of sentencing and judicial decision-making."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: I don't really understand, either through your presentation or the government's, what this \"may\" argument is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Right. But, if Congress wanted these defendants to be able to take advantage of changes in the career offender guideline, I have two thoughts. One, why didn't they say that? And, two, why is that not available to the thousands of other defendants who are out there who are not in the crack/powder situation but could really benefit from the change in the career offender situation, but they're not allowed to? They can't get it under 2255 or 3582, so they're stuck. What about those two things?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 404 of the First Step Act authorizes a limited sentence reduction proceeding, not a plenary resentencing. By its plain terms, Section 404 only requires a district court to take account of one new development, namely, the changes to crack cocaine sentencing made by Sections 2 and 3 of the Fair Sentencing Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner's principal argument, as you've heard this morning, in favor of such an entitlement rests on the term \"impose\" in Section 404, which Petitioner would read to incorporate a requirement to redo the Section 3553(a) analysis that a court does in imposing an initial sentence. That argument is inconsistent with the text of Section 404 as a whole, in particular, with the text of Section 404(c), as well as with the undisputedly limited scope and nature of sentence reduction proceedings."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, you seem fairly certain that there is no -- that Petitioner's argument is foreclosed by the language of the statute. Could you spend a minute on precisely what words you're relying on for this -- for your limitations?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GUARNIERI: Sure. So we do think that there are important limitations in the text of Section 404(b) itself. In particular, Section 404(b) requires the district court to engage in this counterfactual inquiry and to determine the statutory and guidelines range that would have applied to the offender at the time of the original sentencing had Sections 2 and 3 of the Fair Sentencing Act been in effect at that time."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: So you think this language by -- there's sort of a negative implication that nothing --because it mentions this provision, no other considerations can be taken into account?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GUARNIERI: Well, I -- Justice Thomas, I wouldn't say that no other considerations can be taken into account. I -- I would say that the statute does not require the district court to take into account anything else. And that discretionary reading is backstopped by Section 404(c), which says expressly that a sentence reduction is never required in any of these proceedings."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Just to be clear about your position, you're saying that the district court, with respect to changes in the law, is not required to consider the changes in the law but may, is that correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GUARNIERI: That's generally correct, Justice Kavanaugh."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: And -- I want to follow up on that. In what world does it make sense that some district courts will be -- take cognizance of -- of changes in the law like that and others will not, and the results will be, as -- as Justice Kavanaugh points out, dramatically different for different individuals?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Guarnieri",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, as I began to say, this is no different in kind from Rule 35 proceedings under which there is no mandate to redo the 3553(a) analysis. It's -- it's not substantially different than a sentence reduction proceeding under (c)(2), as this Court considered in Dillon."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Kagan, you asked the question of Mr. Guarnieri about the analogous proceeding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Chief Justice, you asked a question about why we shouldn't just fix the one problem and leave it at that, and -- and the problem with that approach is that this is not a mechanical adjustment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0493778531f49b04b3d621e3ca0714e4d7430af55b1ad65b2ad8062b73fe200e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0493778531f4-0004-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In fact, I think 3582(c)(2) helps us because the reason this Court in Dillon did not allow the defendant to make an argument based on current law is because there was explicit text in the policy statement that was at issue there that said you can't raise those sorts of arguments."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1650",
      "confidence": 0.0,
